================================================================
TOPIC 2 — TYPE HINTS AND RUNTIME VALIDATION
CONCEPT 2 — @dataclass
================================================================

CORE PURPOSE
------------
Eliminates boilerplate for data-holding classes. The decorator reads
field annotations at class definition time and auto-generates:
  - __init__
  - __repr__
  - __eq__

BASIC SYNTAX
------------
from dataclasses import dataclass, field

@dataclass
class BatchMetadata:
    source_file: str
    batch_idx: int
    token_count: int
    is_truncated: bool = False
    tags: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)

FIELD ORDERING RULE
-------------------
Fields without defaults must come before fields with defaults.
Violating this raises TypeError at class definition time.

MUTABLE DEFAULTS — CRITICAL RULE
---------------------------------
NEVER use mutable objects as bare defaults:
    tags: list[str] = []        # raises ValueError
    metadata: dict = {}         # raises ValueError
    _cache: dict = field(default={}, repr=False)  # silent shared-state bug

ALWAYS use default_factory:
    tags: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    metadata: dict = field(default_factory=dict)

field() PARAMETERS
------------------
    default         — scalar default value
    default_factory — callable, invoked fresh per instance
    repr=False      — exclude from __repr__
    compare=False   — exclude from __eq__
    init=False      — exclude from __init__, set in __post_init__

frozen=True
-----------
Makes instances immutable after construction.
Raises FrozenInstanceError on attempted mutation.
Also generates __hash__, enabling use as dict keys or set members.
Use for configs — a config that launched a run must never change.

__post_init__
-------------
Runs automatically after __init__. Use for derived/computed fields.
With frozen=True, use object.__setattr__ to bypass the freeze:

    def __post_init__(self) -> None:
        object.__setattr__(self, "run_id", f"{self.model_name}-{uuid4().hex[:8]}")

init=False + __post_init__ PATTERN
-----------------------------------
Standard pattern for auto-generated fields the caller cannot set:

    run_id: str = field(init=False)

    def __post_init__(self) -> None:
        object.__setattr__(self, "run_id", f"{self.model_name}-{uuid4().hex[:8]}")

Place init=False fields last — they are not part of the public
constructor interface.

MODULE-LEVEL FUNCTIONS
----------------------
asdict(instance)              — recursive conversion to dict
astuple(instance)             — recursive conversion to tuple
replace(instance, **changes)  — new instance with changed fields,
                                original untouched
                                NOTE: calls __post_init__ again,
                                so computed fields regenerate

replace() PATTERN FOR EXPERIMENTS
----------------------------------
base = TrainingConfig(model_name="gpt", learning_rate=3e-4)
experimental = replace(base, learning_rate=1e-3)
base is untouched. Standard pattern for hyperparameter sweeps.

WHAT @dataclass DOES NOT DO
----------------------------
Does not enforce types at runtime. This is valid:

    @dataclass
    class Scored:
        item: int
        score: float

    s = Scored(item="wrong", score="also wrong")  # no error

Runtime enforcement requires Pydantic (covered later in Topic 2).

WHEN TO USE
-----------
- Structured data containers you control: batch metadata,
  eval results, checkpoint descriptors
- Configs (frozen=True)
- Anywhere you'd write __init__ + __repr__ + __eq__ by hand

WHEN NOT TO USE
---------------
- Data arriving from outside your code (config files, API responses)
  → use Pydantic instead
- Simple named tuples with no methods → consider NamedTuple
- Complex validation logic at construction time → Pydantic

